src/pages/events/fuel-reserves/2026-10-02-g7-releases-100-million-barrels.astro:231:        <p data-layer="narrative">The IEA's October 2 account puts the {marchSoFar.barrels} million barrels on the March collective action. The October statement does not assign that tally to a portion of its own {release.barrels} barrel timetable. France 24 said 80 percent of overall IEA stocks were "still in our pocket", which is overall stocks, not the unreleased share of the March 400. The German FAQ says the IEA had asked Germany to release 2,6 million tonnes from its reserve, a March request. RTÉ reported that US oil prices dropped sharply, as much as 5% at one point. The diesel series' last row is September 28, and Brent's last row is September 29.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="80 percent of overall IEA stocks “still in our pocket”" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /><Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-29,113.96" /></p>
